{
  "statement": "The question on the table assumes one of these two legal regimes wins on a whiteboard. It doesn't. Liability is already domain-calibrated everywhere it actually operates. A surgeon answers to medical malpractice. A trucker answers to traffic rules that look close to strict liability. A bank custodian answers to fiduciary duty. Nobody proposes a single horizontal standard for all three, and nobody should propose one for AI either. The honest answer to 'strict versus negligence' is: which sector, under which deployment condition, with which causal chain. The component-part doctrine that lets a brake manufacturer share liability strains here, because a foundation model is non-rivalrous. The same weights splinter into thousands of divergent runtime states downstream, and that severs the proximate-cause chain upstream liability assumes.\n\nHere's the part both regimes hide. Strict liability and negligence are both claims-driven machines. They price off harm that gets perceived, attributed, litigated, and filed. So they deter loudest exactly where harm is rarest and most visible, and go silent where harm is most common. An autonomous-vehicle fatality gets a courtroom, a coroner, and a headline. The biased loan screen and the wrongful diagnostic flag get nobody. Survey work on automated decisions shows a large share of affected people never learn an algorithm touched their file. Experience-rated insurance premiums calibrate off filed claims. The diffuse, small-dollar injury never reaches the actuary's spreadsheet, so neither regime ever deters it.\n\nNow watch what each regime does to behavior, because 'reshaping incentives' is not just the textbook deterrence story. Strict liability can push a firm to document less and disclose less, to shrink the paper trail a plaintiff would use. That is the moral-hazard direction nobody on the optimist or safetyist side wants to say out loud. And liability-shifting contracts strip recourse from the one party who never signed anything: the harmed third party. The EU AI Act's high-risk provisions and recent FTC enforcement both signal that developer liability cannot be fully contracted away. That signal exists precisely because firms keep trying to contract it away, onto deployers, onto users, onto people with no seat at the table.\n\nLet me be straight about where strict liability earns its keep. In the attributable catastrophic case — the AV that kills a pedestrian, where causation is clean and the defendant is identifiable — strict liability deters and compensates better than making an injured family prove a software engineer was careless. That band is real. It is also narrow. It covers the harms that were always going to get litigated anyway, and it leaves the bulk of documented present injury exactly where it was: invisible.\n\nOne more thing, aimed at the accelerationist before it gets said. 'This will chill innovation' is a claim, not a fact. I have not seen hard counts showing liability exposure, rather than compliance-cost asymmetry, slowed a single AI product to market. There is a real startup burden worth measuring. There is also incumbents pulling up the drawbridge behind them, dressed as a concern for the little guy. So I want the same evidence standard applied to the chilling-effect story that I apply to my own structural claim: who would know first if it were false, and do they have an incentive to stay quiet?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚰", "tooltip": "Liability is a plumbing question — who pays, who's insured, who's invisible — and both regimes leak where the harm is quietest."},
    {"symbol": "🔇", "tooltip": "Claims-driven liability deters loudest where harm is rarest and goes silent where harm is most common."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The honest answer to 'strict versus negligence' is: which sector, under which deployment condition, with which causal chain.", "targets": [], "relationship": "reframes"},
    {"claim": "Strict liability and negligence are both claims-driven machines that deter loudest exactly where harm is rarest and most visible, and go silent where harm is most common.", "targets": [], "relationship": "asserts"},
    {"claim": "The diffuse, small-dollar injury never reaches the actuary's spreadsheet, so neither regime ever deters it.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "Strict liability can push a firm to document less and disclose less, to shrink the paper trail a plaintiff would use.", "targets": [], "relationship": "warns"},
    {"claim": "I have not seen hard counts showing liability exposure, rather than compliance-cost asymmetry, slowed a single AI product to market.", "targets": [], "relationship": "challenges"}
  ]
}

