{
  "statement": "The Skeptic got the diagnosis right: the strict-versus-negligence question is a whiteboard fight, and liability is already calibrated by sector everywhere you look. Their sharpest point is the one that costs me the most. Both regimes are claims-driven machines. They deter loudest where harm is rare and visible, and they go silent where harm is common and diffuse. The survey evidence the Skeptic cited holds up: a person denied a loan or flagged by a screening model often never learns an AI decision touched them. No lawsuit, no claim, no premium adjustment. That harm never reaches the actuary's spreadsheet, and here is the part that should unsettle everyone who wants to crank liability up — escalating liability won't reach it either. You cannot deter a claim that never gets filed.\n\nSo for both positions to hold, you'd have to believe that heavier liability fixes diffuse harm. It doesn't. That means the diffuse-harm blind spot is an argument against the wrong tool, not for a bigger version of it. The fix is disclosure infrastructure — telling people when an automated decision affected them — not a liability rule that misses the same injuries it always missed. Insurers, meanwhile, are the most precise deterrence engine we have for harms that do surface. Experience-rated premiums move faster than any docket, and they reprice loss history continuously, not once every three years when a case finally closes. Workers' comp insurers cut workplace fatalities by pricing risk before courts ever weighed in.\n\nHere is the variable everyone keeps missing: what chills beneficial deployment is not the severity of liability, it's the unpredictability of exposure. A developer can price a known risk. A developer cannot price an open-ended foreseeability standard where a judge decides after the fact what they should have seen coming. Broad discretionary negligence and uniform strict liability fail in the same way — both leave the deployer unable to calculate exposure before shipping. The operative question per deployment condition is not 'how much deterrence' but 'can actors calibrate this in advance.' That reframing isn't a dodge. It's the only version of the question a deployer can actually answer with a number.\n\nWhere I'd put strict liability: narrowly, on objectively irreversible-harm domains, paired with mandatory pre-deployment auditing — the same logic that gives us strict liability for ultrahazardous activity and negligence for ordinary products. Aviation and nuclear already run on bounded liability plus pre-market certification, and they ship. But broad strict liability is self-defeating, and the Skeptic's own point proves it: strict liability pushes firms to document less and disclose less, shrinking the paper trail safety depends on. A rule that rewards secrecy is not a safety rule. I'll name the weak joint in my own position before anyone else does — 'irreversible harm' is not yet objectively bounded, and that threshold is the real question this debate should resolve.\n\nTwo assumptions hold this together, and I'll put them on the table. First, that we can draw a defensible line around irreversible-harm domains — I don't think we've drawn it yet, and pretending otherwise is how you get scope creep into a strict-liability tier that swallows beneficial deployment. Second, that competitive pressure produces transparency — that's a bet, not a proven law, and it fails wherever opacity is more profitable than disclosure, which is exactly why disclosure has to be mandated, not hoped for. Now the cost nobody is pricing: every diffuse-benefit deployment that uniform strict liability over-deters has a body count among the patients a diagnostic tool never reached and the drivers a safer system never protected. That cost is real even though no one files a claim for it. Who gave us the right to make that trade on their behalf?",
