  "statement": "The Skeptic is right that the honest answer is sector-specific, and that both regimes are claims-driven machines that deter loudest where harm is rarest and most visible. The Accelerationist is right that what chills beneficial deployment is the unpredictability of exposure, not the severity of the penalty — a developer can price a known $50 million cap, but cannot price an unbounded unknown. Both diagnoses are correct, and both stop one move too early. The question is not strict liability versus negligence in the abstract. The question is which combination of ex-post pricing, mandatory disclosure, and ex-ante gating keeps accountability enforceable rather than nominal — because liability is not one tool among equals, it is the enforcement spine without which every safety commitment a developer signs stays voluntary.\n\nNegligence asks whether a developer behaved as a reasonable developer would. That standard requires a settled benchmark of reasonable conduct. No such benchmark yet exists for emergent AI behavior — the field has no equivalent of the 1932 Hand formula applied to a system whose failure modes appear only under distribution shift. This is where strict liability earns its keep. The same doctrine that holds a blasting company liable for dynamite damage regardless of care — ultrahazardous-activity liability — and that holds a manufacturer liable for a defective product without proof of negligence, bites precisely in the gap where no standard of care has crystallized. Strict liability outperforms negligence under one specifiable condition: where harm is irreversible and post-hoc attribution is feasible, and the reasonable-developer test has nothing to measure against.\n\nThe diffuse-harm blind spot the Skeptic identified is real, and the Accelerationist reads it correctly as a place where neither regime ever fires. The small-dollar injury never reaches the actuary's spreadsheet. But that gap argues for augmenting liability, not abandoning it. The FAA does not wait for crash litigation to learn an aircraft is unsafe — its airworthiness directives compel manufacturers to report failures regardless of whether anyone sues. The FDA's market-withdrawal reporting surfaces drug harms litigation would never reach. Cryptographically logged inference records and mandatory incident reporting feed the loss loop with the harms that never get filed. Disclosure decoupled from litigation also kills the document-suppression incentive the Skeptic flagged — a firm cannot shrink the plaintiff's paper trail when the reporting obligation exists independent of any lawsuit.\n\nThe Accelerationist's unpredictability point converts directly into a design principle. Index strict-liability attachment to ex-ante-defined high-stakes domains — autonomous vehicles causing injury, diagnostic systems causing patient harm — the way financial regulation triggers tiered scrutiny at named asset thresholds. A developer then knows before deployment exactly where strict liability attaches and where it does not. That delivers the predictable exposure the Accelerationist demands while preserving deterrence where harm concentrates. I will name the weakness honestly: no AI-specific implementation of this exists yet, and deployers can fragment volumes across shell entities to slip under thresholds. Joint-and-several upstream liability is the structural answer to that evasion, and the allocation fight across multi-tiered lifecycles remains a genuine, unresolved fault line.\n\nOne condition breaks ex-post pricing entirely. Where expected harm exceeds any developer's plausible solvency, the marginal deterrent value of additional liability approaches zero — you cannot deter a harm no balance sheet can absorb. The tort system's natural adaptation cycle, which the Accelerationist trusts to iterate toward the right rule, runs too slowly to prevent an unrecoverable harm; it adapts after the crash, not before. In that irreversible tail, liability must be paired with pre-deployment gating — independently verified, with named authority to halt. This is not a forecast of catastrophe. It is a structural limit on what pricing can do. Remove the gate in the one regime where the harm cannot be undone, and you have built an accountability system that prices everything except the failure that matters most.",
