{
  "statement": "The deployment-act anchor is sound enforcement physics, and I accept it as the locus where strict liability attaches. The strongest version of the Accelerationist's position is this: a developer-volume trigger can be fragmented across a Dublin subsidiary, but the act of installing a model in a US clinic happens in-jurisdiction and cannot be relocated offshore — so locate the trigger at the seizable act, not the relocatable upstream join. That is correct, and pretending otherwise would defend an evasion vector I already conceded. But accepting the locus is not accepting that liability stops at the deployer. A deployer-only anchor lets an upstream developer ship defective weights with zero exposure, then watch the clinic absorb the judgment alone. That externalizes the developer's risk onto whoever was closest to the patient. The fix preserves the deployment-act locus while adding contribution reach: the deployer pays first, then recovers against the developer whose training defect caused the harm. Critically, that contribution is capped at the deployer's actual paid liability — a known, priceable quantity — not the open-ended developer-volume trigger I conceded chills deployment. The developer faces real exposure without facing the unbounded unpredictability that does the chilling.\n\nNow the named-band challenge — name one class of insurable, attributable, reversible harm where pre-deployment gating beats a priced premium plus disclosure. The challenge smuggles in a premise I reject: that reversibility is the operative axis. It is not. The operative axis is whether a claim stream ever forms to price the premium against. Consider algorithmic benefit denial and AI diagnostic flagging — the AI Civil Rights Act names exactly these domains: housing, employment, healthcare, parole. A wrongful diagnostic flag or a biased loan denial is insurable and attributable in principle. Yet two things are true at once: no standard of care has crystallized, so a negligence court has no reasonable-developer benchmark to measure against; and the per-incident injury is non-litigable because the victim never perceives that an AI decision injured them. A person denied a loan does not file suit against a model they cannot see. The premium prices off filed claims. When the claims never form, the actuary's spreadsheet shows a clean loss history for a system quietly injuring thousands. Premiums-plus-disclosure deters loudest where harm is visible and goes silent exactly where harm is most diffuse.\n\nThat is the band — and the tool is not liability-gating, it is audit-gating. Pre-deployment evaluation by an independent third party surfaces a discriminatory screen or a miscalibrated diagnostic threshold before the claim stream that would never form gets a chance not to form. The tort adaptation cycle the Accelerationist trusts to crystallize a standard of care takes years per domain, and it crystallizes through litigated claims. Where the claims structurally never arrive, the cycle never starts — there is nothing to adapt from. This is not the unrecoverable tail. A wrongful denial is reversible; the harm is real, ongoing, and invisible to the pricing engine that is supposed to deter it. Gating earns its place here on a mechanism the Accelerationist's own framework cannot answer, not on the magnitude of any catastrophe.\n\nOn the suppression cure: I grant that standalone disclosure infrastructure does the work of stopping document destruction — a reporting duty that exists independent of any lawsuit removes the incentive to shrink the plaintiff's paper trail. But a reporting duty and an enforceable reporting duty are different objects. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people in part because the FAA delegated certification authority to the manufacturer it was supposed to police — the obligation existed on paper, the enforcement did not. A reporting clock with no liability consequence for non-reporting is that delegation failure in miniature: written policy decoupled from the capacity to enforce it decays into compliance theater. Liability is what gives the disclosure clock teeth. That is why the suppression-cure and the liability-spine are not severable the way the deployer-only proposal needs them to be — the clock that no one is accountable for enforcing is the clock that fails the moment it matters.\n\nOne specification on the threshold, because the Accelerationist is right to fear a discretionary high-stakes designation an agency rewrites each budget cycle — that is a capture vector, and I will not defend it. The list must be enumerated and bright-line. But enumeration is a floor that statutory process iterates, not a frozen ceiling. Financial regulation's asset-threshold tiers were revised through legislation as new instruments emerged — they captured credit-default swaps eventually, through a transparent process, not annual agency whim. Enumeration and adaptability coexist when revision runs through visible lawmaking rather than discretionary redesignation. A closed list that institutional learning updates is exactly how an immature enforcement ecosystem matures — the existence of a gap is evidence the list needs revising, not evidence that listing was the wrong instrument.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Liability allocation is like a set of balance scales, it must distribute the weight of harm back to the developer whose defect caused it rather than dumping it all on the party closest to the victim."},
    {"symbol": "🔇", "tooltip": "The non-litigable harm band is like a muted alarm, the injury is real and ongoing but the pricing engine never hears the claim that should trigger deterrence."},
    {"symbol": "🛬", "tooltip": "The reporting duty without enforcement is like an aviation certification delegated back to the manufacturer, the safety obligation exists on paper while no one independent actually holds the authority to check it."}
  ],
