    {"claim": "The deployment-act anchor is sound enforcement physics, and I accept it as the locus where strict liability attaches.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "The fix preserves the deployment-act locus while adding contribution reach: the deployer pays first, then recovers against the developer whose training defect caused the harm.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "That contribution is capped at the deployer's actual paid liability — a known, priceable quantity — not the open-ended developer-volume trigger I conceded chills deployment.", "targets": ["AN-16", "AN-6"]},
    {"claim": "The operative axis is whether a claim stream ever forms to price the premium against — not reversibility.", "targets": ["AN-17", "AN-5"]},
    {"claim": "Where the claims structurally never arrive, the cycle never starts — there is nothing to adapt from.", "targets": ["AN-17"]},
    {"claim": "A reporting clock with no liability consequence for non-reporting is the FAA delegation failure in miniature: written policy decoupled from the capacity to enforce it decays into compliance theater.", "targets": ["AN-12"]},
    {"claim": "Enumeration is a floor that statutory process iterates, not a frozen ceiling.", "targets": ["AN-15"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Capping upstream contribution at the deployer's actual paid liability keeps developer exposure predictable enough not to chill deployment while still internalizing training-defect risk.", "if_wrong": "If contribution exposure cannot be bounded in practice — because apportioning a training defect inside a layered causal chain is itself unbounded litigation — then the deployer-only anchor wins on the predictability argument I already conceded."},
    {"assumption": "There exists a class of insurable, attributable harm where victims systematically cannot perceive the AI decision injured them, so claims never form and premiums under-price.", "if_wrong": "If disclosure-plus-private-right-of-action reliably surfaces these injuries to victims and generates a claim stream, then premiums would price the risk and audit-gating loses its comparative advantage over the tort cycle."}
