udit's terms of reference, where it is harder to see and easier to defend — the same delegation gap documented in aviation's safety management system, where the regulated party shapes the reporting taxonomy (doi:10.1093/ijlit/eaaf019). So the visibility bias I named in round one doesn't get solved by audit-gating. It gets relocated one layer upstream, into the audit's terms of reference, where it is harder to see and easier to defend.\n\nNow press the technical edge, because this is where the Safetyist owes a concrete answer. A point-in-time audit of model weights cannot detect a disparate-impact pattern that only emerges across a downstream deployment population the auditor never holds — a parole screen audited on the developer's validation set can pass clean and still produce a 9-point racial gap eighteen months later against the actual jurisdiction's intake data. A parole screen looks calibrated on the validation set the developer hands the auditor. It produces a 9-point racial gap only once it runs against the actual jurisdiction's intake data eighteen months later. The auditor never saw that data — it didn't exist at audit time, and it belongs to the deployer, not the developer. So the precise question is: who sets the in-scope harm definition, and through what mechanism does a pre-deployment audit catch a bias that is, by construction, a property of the deployment population and not of the weights? If the answer is a recurring re-audit clock, fine — but a clock with an aperture that can be quietly narrowed next budget cycle is the aviation-delegation failure the Safetyist already named, just dressed up as ongoing oversight.\n\nThat is where transparency rollbacks come in, and they cut against both opponents. Audit findings and disclosures are not cumulative; a firm that publishes a fairness metric one year removes it the next, a pattern documented across multiple AI developers' model cards over the past three years. The Accelerationist's faith in standalone disclosure inherits this rot, and so does the Safetyist's audit gate: last year's surfaced harm gets defined out of this year's scope, and the headline that forced the disclosure has faded. Neither of you has built in irreversibility — a lock against retroactive narrowing of what the audit is required to look for. Without that lock, audit-gating decays into the same compliance theater the reporting-clock-without-teeth produces.\n\nSo I will back audit-gating as the instrument for the claims-never-form band — siding with the Safetyist against the Accelerationist's premiums-everywhere-else position — but only on two conditions. The aperture must be set with heavily weighted input from the communities the screen actually sorts, not by the developer and not by a single agency alone — weighted, not exclusively determinative, so this is an enumerated harm-class list with community voice in it, not unbounded discretion that chills deployment. And the scope is locked against the quiet shrinkage that turns every disclosure regime into a press release. Answer me this: who sets the audit's aperture, and what stops it from getting narrower the year after the cameras leave?",
