    {"claim": "An audit sees only what its scope is built to measure; whoever sets the aperture decides what counts as harm, so visibility bias relocates upstream into the audit's terms of reference rather than dissolving — a structural property independent of bad faith.", "speaker": "Skeptic", "an_id": "AN-24/AN-25", "grounding": [{"node_id": "saf-intentions-225", "why": "PrIME-LLM multi-domain scoring is Safetyist's own apparatus for resisting narrow-aperture gaming; its vulnerability (subjective domain weighting) is exactly the aperture-capture risk Skeptic names, so Safetyist must engage it honestly"}, {"node_id": "cc-120", "why": "the upstream-downstream transparency bifurcation directly frames why an upstream weight-audit cannot capture downstream deployment-population harm"}]},
    {"claim": "A point-in-time audit of model weights cannot detect a disparate-impact pattern that only emerges across a downstream deployment population the auditor never held — the parole screen looks calibrated on the validation set but produces a 9-point racial gap against actual intake data 18 months later.", "speaker": "Skeptic", "an_id": "AN-26", "grounding": [{"node_id": "cc-120", "why": "this is the core safetyist concern that downstream transparency is essential for monitoring real-world harms and upstream-only mechanisms are insufficient"}, {"node_id": "saf-intentions-165", "why": "dual kill criteria including third-party harm complaints provides the post-deployment intake mechanism that recurring re-audit needs — Safetyist's existing answer to population drift"}]},
