    {"claim": "Heavily weighted input from the populations the screen actually sorts is the concrete form of a duty of care owed to affected third parties who never signed the deployment contract — but 'weighted, not determinative' leaves a genuine aggregation problem unsolved.", "targets": ["AN-28"]},
    {"claim": "Audit-gating belongs precisely where the injury is a property of the population rather than a discrete event the victim can name — that is the band where the claim stream never forms.", "targets": ["AN-24", "AN-25"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Logged inference records and held deployment-population data can be made trustworthy enough to audit against — that the deployer who controls the logging cannot selectively curate what the re-audit clock sees.", "if_wrong": "If the deployer can game the logging, the re-audit clock inherits the same visibility bias one layer down, and cryptographic attestation of the inference log becomes a hard design prerequisite rather than an assumption — the proposal does not bind without it."},
    {"assumption": "A liability consequence attached to lapsed or narrowed re-audit scope actually changes deployer behavior, rather than being absorbed as a priced cost of doing business in a band where the harm exceeds detection.", "if_wrong": "If liability for scope-shrinkage is too small or too rarely enforced, the scope-lock decays exactly as the standalone disclosure regime does, and the FAA-delegation failure mode returns despite the statutory lock."}
  ],
