    {"label": "Mandating Population-Held Continuous Re-Audits", "description": "An Intention within safetyist discourse to replace one-shot pre-deployment certification with recurring third-party audits run against live deployment-population data and cryptographically logged inference records.\nEncompasses: periodic re-audit clocks, held-out deployment-population testing, cryptographic inference logging, standing harm-complaint intake channels, statutory scope-locks against retroactive narrowing.\nExcludes: one-time weight-audits on vendor validation sets, voluntary fairness self-reporting, and post-hoc litigation discovery.", "pov": "safetyist", "category": "Intentions", "rationale": "The debate's central late-stage convergence was that disparate impact is a property of the model-population interaction emerging months after deployment, which no point-in-time weight-audit can catch — a mechanism existing audit-mandate nodes do not capture because they assume development-time inspection.", "source_claims": ["C23", "C26", "C27"]},
    {"label": "Claims-Stream Formation as Deterrence Precondition", "description": "A Belief that liability and insurance regimes deter only where a perceivable, attributable, litigable claim stream forms, and structurally fail where harm is diffuse and invisible regardless of penalty severity.\nEncompasses: visibility bias in tort and insurance, the non-litigability of aggregate statistical harm, the actuary-spreadsheet blind spot for unfiled injury, disparate-impact harms no individual can name.\nExcludes: claims about penalty magnitude, reversibility thresholds, and developer solvency limits.", "pov": "skeptic", "category": "Beliefs", "rationale": "All three debaters converged on this as the load-bearing axis displacing both 'severity' and 'reversibility,' yet it is a distinct structural mechanism — deterrence keyed to whether a claim ever forms — that no existing belief node names.", "source_claims": ["C1", "C2", "C20s", "C21", "C27"]},
    {"label": "Anchoring Liability to the Deployment Act", "description": "An Intention to locate the legal trigger at the in-jurisdiction act of deploying a model rather than at upstream developer volume, treating deployment as a seizable, unfragmentable chokepoint.\nEncompasses: deployment-act anchors, jurisdictional chokepoint enforcement, anti-shell-fragmentation triggers, deployer-first-payment with capped developer contribution.\nExcludes: develop","evaluate":"{
