      "rationale": "The skeptic (C23, C25) lands a real empirical hit — model cards show fairness metrics appear one year and vanish the next, proving audit scope is not cumulative without a lock. But the safetyist's C26b reframe absorbs that hit by making the irreversibility lock load-bearing through a liability consequence for letting the clock lapse, which directly answers the 'scope quietly narrows once cameras leave' mechanism. The skeptic identifies the failure mode; the safetyist specifies the only instrument (statutory non-shrinkability tied to liability) that closes it, and the skeptic offers no rebuttal showing why a liability-backed lock still decays.",
      "what_would_change_this": "Evidence that statutorily locked, liability-backed audit apertures in an existing regime (e.g., HMDA fair-lending audits or EEOC adverse-impact testing) still get gamed by narrowing terms of reference would flip this toward the skeptic."
    },
    {
      "conflict": "Whether the negligence standard crystallizes fast enough for the reversible, insurable majority — making strict liability pure deadweight chill outside the unrecoverable tail.",
      "claim_ids": ["C21", "C8", "C20", "C27"],
      "prevails": "C20",
      "criterion": "logical_validity",
      "rationale": "The accelerationist's reversibility axis (C8, C21) assumes a claim stream exists to crystallize from, but the safetyist's reframe (C20, C27) shows that assumption fails in the diffuse band where C2's uncontested evidence establishes victims never learn an AI touched their file. If no claim ever forms, the negligence cycle never starts, so 'crystallizes fast enough' is not even false — it is inapplicable. The accelerationist's C9 body-count point about over-deterrence is real but lives in a different band (visible, attributable harm) and does not rescue the reversibility axis for diffuse harm.",
      "what_would_change_this": "Survey or docket evidence that diffuse algorithmic harms (loan denials, screening flags) actually do generate filed claims at material rates — via class actions, regulator referrals, or mandatory disclosure — would restore the accelerationist's crystallization argument."
    },
    {
      "conflict": "Whether liability should reach upstream developers at all, given the deployment-act anchor — accelerationist's deployer-only locus versus safetyist's capped recovery against developers.",
      "claim_ids": ["C18", "C22", "C16", "C18"],
      "prevails": "C22",
      "criterion": "specificity",
      "rationale": "The accelerationist's deployment-act anchor (C18) wins the anti-fragmentation point decisively — you cannot split putting a diagnostic model into a US clinic across a Dublin subsidiary — and that defeats the safetyist's volume-trigger version (C16). But C22 concedes the deployment-act locus and bolts on a more precise mechanism: deployer pays first, then recovers against the developer capped at the deployer's actual paid liability, converting the accelerationist's feared open-ended exposure into a known, priceable quantity. C22 is the more specific and testable instrument because it bounds developer exposure to a number that already exists post-judgment, and the accelerationist leaves C22 formally unanswered.",
