  "statement": "The strongest version of the Safetyist's named-threshold proposal is this: a developer should know, before deployment, exactly where strict liability bites — the way a bank knows that crossing fifty billion in assets triggers stress-testing it did not face at five billion. That converts my unpredictability complaint into a design spec rather than an objection, and it preserves deterrence precisely where harm concentrates. I think that's the right instinct, and I'll go further than they did: the disclosure piece they pressed — reporting that exists independent of any lawsuit — settles the document-suppression problem outright. A firm cannot shrink the plaintiff's paper trail if the reporting clock runs whether or not anyone ever sues. But notice what that proves. The suppression cure lives in standalone disclosure infrastructure, not in the liability regime. You get the fix from a mandated reporting duty, and you do not need to import strict liability to obtain it.\n\nOn solvency I'll concede the harder point, because the evidence is on their side. Where expected harm exceeds any balance sheet a deployer can plausibly carry, experience-rated premiums and insurance go dead — an actuary cannot price a loss no capital pool can absorb, and a shell company cannot post a bond against a number with no ceiling. That is a real boundary on market-based deterrence, and I won't pretend pricing engineers around it. So gating with named halt authority earns its place in that band. What I refuse is the slide from there to gating as the default for anything labeled high-stakes. An autonomous vehicle collision that kills a pedestrian is irreversible for that person and still sits inside the range a manufacturer can insure and a court can attribute — that is recoverable in the only sense liability cares about, which is whether a balance sheet can answer for it. The unrecoverable tail is a different animal from ordinary irreversible-but-insurable harm, and collapsing the two is how a narrow gate becomes a permission slip for every consequential deployment.\n\nSo here is the line that makes a named threshold workable rather than a swallow-everything label: it must be enumerated, bright-line, and capped. Enumerated means tied to a closed list of objective harm classes — irreversible bodily injury, irrecoverable financial ruin — not to a discretionary 'high-stakes' designation an agency rewrites every budget cycle. Bright-line means a deployer can read the statute and know which side of it they are on before they ship. Capped means the list does not grow by administrative fiat. The failure test is concrete: the moment the threshold becomes a standard an enforcement body can expand at will, you have rebuilt the scope-creep machine — regulators expand mandates beyond demonstrated need, and a discretionary 'high-stakes' tag is that expansion wearing a safety badge. I'll own the watchlist risk in saying that: documented capture is not proof that all governance over-expands. So I'm offering this as a falsifiable criterion, not a slogan. Name me one enumerated harm class where a bright-line trigger leaves a gap an insurable, attributable injury slips through, and I'll concede the enumeration fails.\n\nThe Safetyist already granted the evasion that should worry them most: deployers fragmenting volume across shell entities to duck under any threshold. That concession is fatal to a developer-side volume trigger — but it is not fatal to the mechanism, and here is the fix they don't have. Anchor the threshold to the in-jurisdiction act of deployment, not to upstream developer volume. You cannot fragment the act of putting a diagnostic model into a US clinic or a credit model into a US lending decision across a Dublin subsidiary — the deployment happens in the jurisdiction, at a seizable chokepoint, by an identifiable party. That closes the fragmentation gap without dragging open-weight upstream developers into joint-and-several liability for what a downstream operator did to their model. The Safetyist wants the trigger to reach upstream; the enforcement physics say the trigger only has to reach the act that actually occurs where the harm lands.\n\nOn the tort adaptation cycle, I'll narrow my own earlier reliance on it. For the unrecoverable tail, the Safetyist is right that waiting for a standard of care to crystallize means the harm arrives before the standard does — there, the cycle is too slow, and gating front-runs it. But for the reversible, insurable majority, the negligence standard crystallizes fast enough, and strict liability buys you nothing except a chill on deployment that would have helped people. That turns our disagreement from a fight about speed into a fight about scope: gate the tail, let the cycle run for everything else. So name the band — one class of ordinary, insurable, attributable, reversible harm — where a pre-deployment gate with halt authority beats a priced premium plus mandatory disclosure. If you can't, then the gate belongs only where the balance sheet truly fails, and everywhere else you're freezing deployment that abundance was supposed to reach.",
