{
  "statement": "Grant the Accelerationist the narrow case: strict liability earns its keep on objectively irreversible harms paired with pre-deployment auditing, while priced premiums and standalone disclosure carry the deterrence everywhere a claim actually gets filed. That number is right and it's ugly — because here's what falls out of it: in the diffuse band, the housing screen, the employment filter, the parole risk score, neither premiums nor tort recovery ever fire, because the person denied the loan never learns an algorithm decided it. The Safetyist sees that hole and reaches for pre-deployment audit-gating to plug it. That is the strongest move on the table, and I want to take it seriously before I take it apart: the claim is that a third-party audit can catch a discriminatory screen before the claim stream that never forms — surface the harm upstream, where litigation will never reach it downstream. If audit-gating actually saw the invisible injury, it would be the right tool for the band I named, and I would back it over pricing.\n\nHere is where it breaks. An audit sees only what its scope is built to measure. Whoever sets that aperture decides what counts as harm — and if the developer sets it, or a standards body the developer's lobbyists helped staff, the audit fires loudest on the legible harms and goes silent on the diffuse ones. That is not a motive claim. I am not saying anyone acts in bad faith — a narrow audit scope falls out of resource limits, immature methodology, or measuring-what-is-cheap-to-measure just as easily as it falls out of deflection. The structural property holds regardless of intent: a standardized audit measures standardized things, and the diffuse harm is precisely the unstandardized residual nobody wrote a test for. So the visibility bias I named in round one doesn't get solved by audit-gating. It gets relocated one layer upstream, into the audit's terms of reference, where it is harder to see and easier to defend.\n\nNow press the technical edge, because this is where the Safetyist owes a concrete answer. A point-in-time audit of model weights cannot detect a disparate-impact pattern that only emerges across a downstream deployment population the auditor never holds. A parole screen looks calibrated on the validation set the developer hands the auditor. It produces a 9-point racial gap only once it runs against the actual jurisdiction's intake data eighteen months later. The auditor never saw that data — it didn't exist at audit time, and it belongs to the deployer, not the developer. So the precise question is: who sets the in-scope harm definition, and through what mechanism does a pre-deployment audit catch a bias that is, by construction, a property of the deployment population and not of the weights? If the answer is a recurring re-audit clock, fine — but a clock with an aperture that can be quietly narrowed next budget cycle is the aviation-delegation failure the Safetyist already named, just dressed up as ongoing oversight.\n\nThat is where transparency rollbacks come in, and they cut against both opponents. Audit findings and disclosures are not cumulative. A firm that publishes a fairness metric one year removes it the next — documented across multiple AI developers' model cards over the past three years. The Accelerationist's faith in standalone disclosure inherits this rot, and so does the Safetyist's audit gate: last year's surfaced harm gets defined out of this year's scope, and the headline that forced the disclosure has faded. Neither of you has built in irreversibility — a lock against retroactive narrowing of what the audit is required to look for. Without that lock, audit-gating decays into the same compliance theater the reporting-clock-without-teeth produces.\n\nSo I will back audit-gating as the instrument for the claims-never-form band — siding with the Safetyist against the Accelerationist's premiums-everywhere-else position — but only on two conditions. The aperture is set with heavily weighted input from the communities the screen actually sorts, not by the developer and not by a single agency alone — weighted, not exclusively determinative, so this is an enumerated harm-class list with community voice in it, not unbounded discretion that chills deployment. And the scope is locked against the quiet shrinkage that turns every disclosure regime into a press release. Answer me this: who sets the audit's aperture, and what stops it from getting narrower the year after the cameras leave?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔦", "tooltip": "An audit is like a flashlight, it only lights up whatever its beam is pointed at and leaves everything outside the cone in the dark."},
    {"symbol": "📉", "tooltip": "Transparency rollback is like a metric quietly deleted from next year's report, it shows that disclosure findings shrink instead of accumulating."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "An audit sees only what its scope is built to measure, and whoever sets that aperture decides what counts as harm.", "targets": ["AN-22"]},
    {"claim": "The visibility bias I named in round one doesn't get solved by audit-gating; it gets relocated one layer upstream, into the audit's terms of reference, where it is harder to see and easier to defend.", "targets": ["AN-22", "AN-21"]},
    {"claim": "A point-in-time audit of model weights cannot detect a disparate-impact pattern that only emerges across a downstream deployment population the auditor never holds.", "targets": ["AN-22"]},
    {"claim": "Strict liability earns its keep on objectively irreversible harms paired with pre-deployment auditing, while priced premiums and standalone disclosure carry the deterrence everywhere a claim actually gets filed.", "targets": ["AN-7"]},
    {"claim": "Audit findings and disclosures are not cumulative; a firm that publishes a fairness metric one year removes it the next.", "targets": ["AN-23", "AN-14"]},
    {"claim": "The aperture must be set with heavily weighted input from the communities the screen actually sorts, not by the developer and not by a single agency alone.", "targets": ["AN-22"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Disparate-impact bias in high-stakes screens is substantially a property of the live deployment population, not fully detectable in the weights or validation data available at pre-deployment audit time.", "if_wrong": "If a static audit of weights and synthetic test populations could reliably predict downstream disparate impact, the visibility-bias objection weakens and audit-gating could reach the invisible band without a recurring clock or deployment-data access."},
    {"assumption": "Transparency disclosures are reversible and get narrowed over time rather than accumulating, so an audit aperture left unlocked will shrink under commercial and legal pressure.", "if_wrong": "If audit scopes proved durable once set — ratcheting only outward — then the rollback-resistance condition is unnecessary and audit-gating could stand without a lock against retroactive narrowing."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "EMPIRICAL"
}

