{
  "statement": "The Skeptic's question deserves a direct answer, and the strongest version of it is this: a pre-deployment audit measures the model the auditor holds in their hands, but disparate impact is not a property of the weights — it is a property of the interaction between the model and a specific deployment population the auditor never sees. A parole screen can clear every fairness check on the vendor's validation set and still open a nine-point racial gap eighteen months later, because the intake population in that jurisdiction differs from the validation distribution. That is correct, and it is fatal to one-shot certification. A weight-audit anchored upstream cannot capture a harm that materializes downstream in usage data — the bifurcation between what we measure at development and what actually happens at deployment is exactly where the injury hides. I am not going to defend a one-time gate against that objection, because the objection wins.\n\nThe gap is the argument for continuous monitoring, not against gating. The cure for a harm that emerges in deployment-population data is a recurring re-audit clock that runs against held deployment-population data — cryptographically logged inference records plus a standing third-party harm-complaint intake channel — not a single certificate issued before the model ever meets the population it will sort. When the parole screen's racial gap appears in intake data, the re-audit clock is the only instrument positioned to catch it, because it is the only one holding the data where the gap lives. A one-shot weight-audit asks 'is this model calibrated against a sample.' A recurring re-audit asks 'is this model producing equitable outcomes against the actual people it is deciding.' The Skeptic's drift objection does not refute audit-gating; it specifies what the gate has to be — periodic, population-held, and live.\n\nThe transparency-rollback the Skeptic documents — a firm publishing a fairness metric one year and quietly deleting it the next across multiple developers' model cards — is the same decay I named against standalone disclosure, and the two critiques converge on a single load-bearing point. A scope-lock with no liability consequence for shrinking it is a reporting clock with no teeth, and it rots into the FAA's delegation failure: written policy decoupled from the authority to enforce it becomes theater, and 346 people died at Boeing because a certification duty was delegated to the party it was meant to constrain. The statutory irreversibility lock the Skeptic demands is not a competing instrument to the liability spine I have argued for — it IS that spine. A non-shrinkable audit scope binds only because a deployer who lets the clock lapse, or who quietly narrows what the re-audit measures, faces a liability consequence for the harm that the lapse let through. Both opponents arrived at the same place from opposite directions: the lock requires teeth, and the teeth are liability.\n\nThe community-weighted aperture the Skeptic proposes is the right answer to who decides what the re-audit measures, and I accept it — heavily weighted input from the populations the screen actually sorts is the concrete form of a duty of care owed to affected third parties who never signed the deployment contract. The applicant denied parole is not the deployer's customer; their interest enters the aperture only if the people subject to the screen have weighted standing to shape what it looks for. I will not pretend this dissolves the capture problem cleanly. 'Weighted, not determinative' leaves a genuine aggregation problem unsolved: when the priorities of different affected groups conflict, no formula tells you how to weigh them against each other, and my own framework flags that stakeholder orderings can be incommensurable. That is a shared open question, not a win for either of us — but it is a far better failure mode than a developer setting the aperture alone, because at least the people sorted by the screen are in the room when its terms are written.\n\nThe boundary that decides where audit-gating attaches is not the domain label — it is whether the injured party can detect and attribute the harm in time to file. A wrongful loan denial may eventually reach a courtroom; it is slow to litigate, but a claim stream forms. The nine-point racial gap is structurally different: no single applicant experiences it, because it is a statistical property of the aggregate that no individual claimant can see, attribute, or sue over. Audit-gating belongs precisely where the injury is a property of the population rather than a discrete event the victim can name — that is the band where the claim stream never forms, and where, absent a population-held re-audit clock with a liability consequence behind it, the gap surfaces eighteen months late with no one ever obligated to have been watching.",
